{
  "generated_text": "To solve the problem, we need to use the properties of a trapezoid and the fact that the angles form an arithmetic sequence. Let's denote the angles of the trapezoid as \\(a\\), \\(a+d\\), \\(a+2d\\), and \\(a+3d\\), where \\(a\\) is the smallest angle and \\(d\\) is the common difference of the arithmetic sequence.\n\nGiven that the smallest angle \\(a = 60^\\circ\\), we can substitute this value into the sequence:\n\\[a = 60^\\circ, \\quad a+d = 60^\\circ + d, \\quad a+2d = 60^\\circ + 2d, \\quad a+3d = 60^\\circ + 3d.\\]\n\nThe sum of the interior angles of a trapezoid is \\(360^\\circ\\). Therefore, we can write the equation:\n\\[60^\\circ + (60^\\circ + d) + (60^\\circ + 2d) + (60^\\circ + 3d) = 360^\\circ.\\]\n\nSimplifying the left side, we get:\n\\[60^\\circ + 60^\\circ + 60^\\circ + 60^\\circ + d + 2d + 3d = 360^\\circ,\\]\n\\[240^\\circ + 6d = 360^\\circ.\\]\n\nNext, we solve for \\(d\\):\n\\[6d = 360^\\circ - 240^\\circ,\\]\n\\[6d = 120^\\circ,\\]\n\\[d = 20^\\circ.\\]\n\nNow that we have the common difference \\(d = 20^\\circ\\), we can find the largest angle, which is \\(a + 3d\\):\n\\[a + 3d = 60^\\circ + 3 \\cdot 20^\\circ = 60^\\circ + 60^\\circ = 120^\\circ.\\]\n\nThus, the measure of the largest angle is \\(\\boxed{120}\\).",
